---
import PublicationLayout from '../../layouts/PublicationLayout.astro';
import { PUBLICATIONS } from '../../data/publications';

const item = PUBLICATIONS.find((entry) => entry.href === '/engineering/agricolae-regw-treatment-labels/')!;
const example = "library(agricolae)\ny <- rep(c(0, 13/8, 4, 8), each=6) + rep(c(-1, 1), 12)\nrun <- function(labels) {\n  d <- data.frame(y=y, trt=factor(rep(labels, each=6)))\n  REGW.test(aov(y ~ trt, data=d), \"trt\", group=FALSE)$comparison\n}\nrun(c(\"A\", \"B\", \"C\", \"D\"))[\"A - B\", ]\nrun(c(\"A\", \"D\", \"C\", \"B\"))[\"A - D\", ]";
---

<PublicationLayout
  category={item.category}
  type={item.type}
  title={item.title}
  description={item.summary}
  published={item.date}
  status={item.status}
  toc={[]}
>
  <section id="finding">
    <h2>The names change; the observations do not</h2>
    <p>
      Treatment names identify groups in an experiment. Renaming them should leave
      the comparison of the same observations unchanged. In agricolae, an R package for analysing experiments,
      <code>REGW.test()</code> compares treatment groups. In our example, swapping
      two group names changes a
      reported p-value from <code>0.0363</code> to <code>0.0791</code>. The first
      result is below the 5% significance threshold; the second is above it.
    </p>
    <p>
      The displayed interval and the letters used to summarize group differences
      also change. Every observation remains in the same numeric group. The effect
      comes from renaming the groups, without adding, removing or changing data.
    </p>
    <div class="notice">
      <p>
        Tasuku Kobayashi sent the report and reproduction files to the agricolae
        maintainer by email on September 10, 2026. <strong>{item.status}.</strong>
        The submission date is recorded by the sender. There is no public upstream
        discussion or accepted fix to link to for this report; the technical
        materials below are published by Licklider for readers to inspect and run.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="example">
    <h2>A small example crosses the 5% threshold</h2>
    <p>
      The example has four groups of six observations, with means of 0, 1.625, 4
      and 8. Within each group, observations alternate one unit below and above
      its mean. We first name the groups A, B, C and D, then exchange the names
      B and D. The first two numeric groups are therefore compared as A–B before
      renaming and A–D afterward.
    </p>
    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table>
        <caption>The same numeric pair before and after renaming</caption>
        <thead><tr><th>Group labels</th><th>Compared pair</th><th>p-value</th><th>Displayed lower bound</th><th>Displayed upper bound</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>A, B, C, D</td><td>A–B</td><td>0.0363</td><td>−3.15369694116</td><td>−0.0963030588423</td></tr>
          <tr><td>A, D, C, B</td><td>A–D</td><td>0.0791</td><td>−3.39520265094</td><td>0.145202650939</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      The difference between the compared means is −1.625 in both cases. Both
      fitted models have 20 residual degrees of freedom and residual mean
      square 1.2. The first displayed interval excludes zero; the second includes it.
      With <code>group=TRUE</code>, the same two groups change from distinct letters
      to a shared letter.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="reproduction">
    <h2>Run the reported comparison</h2>
    <p>
      The recorded environment is CRAN agricolae <code>1.3-7</code> on R
      <code>4.6.1</code>, Linux x86_64. R prints the package version as
      <code>1.3.7</code>. The example uses the documented model interface, so the
      function extracts the model’s error parameters itself.
    </p>
    <pre><code>{example}</code></pre>
    <p>
      The full script checks that the compared observations are identical after
      renaming, prints both pairwise comparisons and group displays, and records
      the R/package versions and model error parameters. It makes four REGW calls
      using an unmodified installed package, with no random inputs or external data.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><a href="/reports/agricolae-regw-labels/reproduce.R" download>reproduce.R</a> — standalone reproduction script.</li>
      <li><a href="/reports/agricolae-regw-labels/observed-output.txt" download>observed-output.txt</a> — captured execution output.</li>
      <li><a href="/reports/agricolae-regw-labels/README.md" download>README.md</a> — execution instructions, environment and preparation disclosure.</li>
      <li><a href="/reports/agricolae-regw-labels/report.md" download>report.md</a> — report title and body.</li>
      <li><a href="/reports/agricolae-regw-labels/SHA256SUMS" download>SHA256SUMS</a> — hashes for the four files above.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>With agricolae installed, run <code>Rscript --vanilla reproduce.R</code> in a clean session.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="source">
    <h2>The apparent cause is an ordering by labels</h2>
    <p>
      In the <a href="https://github.com/cran/agricolae/blob/06e4e366bff13eb0c651cb9b086384c5487b356a/R/REGW.test.R">inspected source</a>,
      <code>Omeans &lt;- order(means[, 1], decreasing = TRUE)</code> orders the first
      column, which contains treatment labels. The response means are in the
      second column. The resulting ranks affect the range size used for p-values
      and displayed intervals.
    </p>
    <p>
      For the reported pair, the span in label order changes from two to four
      while its span in mean order stays two. The report asks whether this ordering
      should use the response means. That identifies a source location to inspect;
      it does not establish a complete correction to the REGW procedure.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="scope">
    <h2>What this finding establishes</h2>
    <p>
      The check is a simple invariance requirement: a one-to-one renaming should
      preserve inference for the same observations and group membership. Neither
      observed p-value is treated as a certified reference answer. This example
      does not estimate how often the issue occurs, prove the procedure’s overall
      false-positive control, or validate a production patch.
    </p>
    <p>
      For Licklider, the work demonstrates how a small reproducible example and
      source inspection can expose a consequential change in statistical output.
      It does not add REGW verification to nomue. See the
      <a href="/docs/limits/">current verification scope</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      The report and reproducer were prepared with AI assistance. The published
      observations come from native R execution. Upstream confirmation and any
      eventual correction will be recorded separately from this reproduction.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="references">
    <h2>Package documentation and reporting route</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><a href="https://cran.r-project.org/package=agricolae">agricolae on CRAN</a> — package version and maintainer information.</li>
      <li><a href="https://search.r-project.org/CRAN/refmans/agricolae/html/REGW.test.html">REGW.test documentation</a> — the supported model-input interface.</li>
      <li><a href="https://www.r-project.org/bugs.html">R’s bug-reporting guidance</a> — directs contributed-package reports to their maintainer when no dedicated bug-reporting page is specified.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</PublicationLayout>

